  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively point toward composition or microstructure engineering of refractory BCC alloys and toward using a second-phase architecture to improve strength–ductility balance. Most importantly, R4 reports a BCC-plus-Laves microstructure in the closely related HfNbTiVZr system, while R1, R3, R7, R12, R16, and R18 show that appropriately distributed intermetallic or heterogeneous second phases can strengthen alloys without necessarily destroying plasticity. Several records also support solid-solution/lattice-friction or dislocation-based strengthening. However, none identifies dilute Cr addition to HfMoTaTiZr, the specific 4.2 at.% level, formation of Cr2(Ti,Zr,Hf,Ta), or a reticulated Laves network that hinders cracks and compensates plasticity. Thus the literature points in the right direction, but selecting Cr and engineering the particular network morphology would require a substantial new idea."
}
